Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "learned_early_stopping_bfgs_docking",
  "Title": "Ligand Viability Prediction: Learned Early Stopping for BFGS Local Search in GPU-Accelerated Molecular Docking",
  "Short Hypothesis": "A lightweight classifier trained on BFGS optimization trajectories can predict, as early as iteration 10–20, whether a ligand will ultimately achieve a favorable binding energy, enabling early termination of unpromising ligands and yielding 2–4× speedup in virtual screening with negligible loss in enrichment factor. This is distinct from static complexity-based exhaustiveness heuristics because it leverages the actual optimization dynamics — energy trajectory, gradient norm decay, and step-size patterns — to make runtime decisions.",
  "Related Work": "Existing work on adaptive docking (e.g., AutoDock Vina's exhaustiveness parameter, QVina's adaptive search depth) adjusts the global search budget based on static ligand properties such as torsion count or atom count. Recent work on learning to optimize (L2O) has applied neural networks to predict step sizes or stopping criteria for convex optimization problems, but has not been applied to non-convex BFGS local search in molecular docking. Uni-Dock introduced batch-mode processing but treats all ligands identically. The proposed method is fundamentally different: it learns from the optimization trajectory itself to make per-ligand stopping decisions, akin to predictive early stopping in hyperparameter optimization but applied at the level of individual BFGS iterations within a docking run.",
  "Abstract": "GPU-accelerated molecular docking has dramatically reduced per-ligand computation time, yet the BFGS local search remains the dominant cost, consuming 85–90% of runtime. Critically, the majority of ligands in a virtual screening library are non-binders that will ultimately be discarded — yet they receive the same computational budget as true actives. We propose Ligand Viability Prediction (LVP), a learned early-stopping mechanism that monitors the BFGS optimization trajectory in real time and predicts, using a lightweight gradient-boosted classifier, whether the current ligand is likely to achieve a binding energy below a user-specified threshold. The classifier is trained on thousands of complete BFGS trajectories collected from CPU Vina runs on diverse protein-ligand pairs, using features extracted at each iteration: current energy, energy delta, gradient norm, ligand torsional degrees of freedom, and the local curvature estimate from the BFGS inverse Hessian. At inference time, the classifier is evaluated every N iterations (e.g., N=10) during GPU docking; if the predicted probability of success falls below a calibrated threshold, the optimization is terminated and the ligand is assigned a poor score. We evaluate LVP on the CASF-2016 screening benchmark (57 targets × 285 ligands), measuring speedup versus enrichment factor retention at EF@1% and EF@5%. We further investigate whether the classifier generalizes across protein targets or requires per-target fine-tuning, and analyze the impact of classifier latency on GPU utilization. This approach is orthogonal to existing acceleration techniques (batch mode, mixed precision, RILC-BFGS) and can be combined with any GPU-accelerated Vina variant to further improve virtual screening throughput.",
  "Experiments": "1. **Trajectory Dataset Collection**: Run Vina-CPU on 10,000+ ligands across 50 diverse protein targets from the PDBbind refined set, logging full BFGS trajectories: at each iteration, record energy, gradient norm, step size, number of function evaluations, and final converged energy. Label each trajectory as 'hit' (final energy < −7.5 kcal/mol) or 'miss'. 2. **Feature Engineering and Classifier Training**: Extract per-iteration features: current energy (E_t), energy improvement (E_t − E_{t−1}), gradient L2 norm, ratio of gradient norm to initial gradient norm, iteration index, number of rotatable bonds, molecular weight. Train XGBoost classifier to predict 'hit' at iterations {5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 40, 50}. Evaluate AUC and recall-at-95%-precision on held-out ligands and held-out proteins to assess generalization. 3. **Integration into GPU Docking Pipeline**: Modify QuickVina2-CUDA (fastest GPU variant with RILC-BFGS) to invoke the classifier every 10 iterations. If P(hit) < 0.1, terminate BFGS and assign a default poor score. Measure wall-clock time and GPU utilization impact. 4. **Screening Power Evaluation**: Run the full CASF-2016 screening benchmark (57 targets × 285 ligands) with and without LVP. Compute EF@1%, EF@5%, and ROC-AUC for each target. Measure total wall-clock time. Report speedup vs. enrichment factor trade-off curves. 5. **Ablation Studies**: (a) Compare learned stopping vs. static energy-threshold stopping (e.g., terminate if E > −5 after 10 iterations). (b) Test per-target vs. global classifier. (c) Vary the probability threshold to map the full speed-accuracy Pareto frontier. (d) Measure the overhead of classifier inference on GPU throughput in batch mode. 6. **Transferability Test**: Evaluate the classifier trained on PDBbind on the CASF-2016 targets to measure zero-shot generalization. If needed, fine-tune with 10–20 trajectories from the new target (few-shot adaptation).",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1. The classifier may overfit to the energy distribution of training targets and fail to generalize to novel protein families with different binding site characteristics. Mitigation: evaluate on held-out protein clusters and use domain-invariant features. 2. Early termination might miss 'slow starters' — ligands that have poor initial energy but converge to a good pose after many iterations. Mitigation: calibrate the probability threshold conservatively and analyze false-negative cases. 3. The classifier adds CPU-side inference overhead that could stall the GPU pipeline, especially in batch mode where many ligands are processed simultaneously. Mitigation: use batched classifier inference on GPU (via cuML or PyTorch C++ API) to keep computation on-device. 4. The optimal stopping threshold may depend on the specific enrichment metric prioritized (e.g., EF@1% favors early enrichment, which may be more sensitive to false negatives). Mitigation: provide tunable thresholds and report full trade-off curves. 5. The method relies on Vina's scoring function being reasonably calibrated; if the scoring function itself is poor for a given target, early stopping decisions will be unreliable."
}}" }
