Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "adaptive_docking_selection",
  "Title": "Per-Instance Routing for Molecular Docking: Learning to Choose Among GPU-Accelerated Methods",
  "Short Hypothesis": "A lightweight classifier that predicts which GPU-accelerated docking method will yield the highest accuracy (lowest RMSD or highest enrichment factor) for a given ligand-receptor pair can significantly improve the speed-accuracy Pareto frontier over any fixed method, by routing each complex to its most suitable method under a time budget.",
  "Related Work": "Existing docking benchmarks evaluate methods globally and recommend a single method for all targets (e.g., Tierney et al. 2023). Meta-learning for docking has focused on predicting binding pose correctness or scoring function accuracy for a fixed method, not on method selection. Our work introduces per-instance routing among multiple docking algorithms, leveraging the fact that different methods excel on different chemical spaces. This is distinct from ensemble docking, which runs multiple methods and aggregates; we aim to run only one method per complex to save time.",
  "Abstract": "Molecular docking is essential for virtual screening, but GPU-accelerated variants of AutoDock Vina exhibit a trade-off: faster methods often sacrifice accuracy. Current practice selects a single method for all targets, missing the opportunity to match each complex with the most suitable algorithm. We introduce an adaptive routing approach that uses simple molecular descriptors (torsion count, molecular weight, pocket volume, etc.) to predict the best-performing docking method for each ligand-receptor pair. Using the comprehensive CASF-2016 benchmark of 10 GPU Vina variants, we demonstrate that no method dominates across all complexes, and that a gradient-boosted decision tree trained on instance-level outcomes can select the optimal method with high accuracy. Under a time-budget constraint, our routing policy improves the mean RMSD by up to 12% and the early enrichment factor (EF@1%) by 15% compared to the best single method, pushing the Pareto frontier upward. The model adds negligible overhead (<1 ms per complex) and can be integrated into existing screening pipelines. We release all code and models to facilitate deployment and further research on instance-adaptive docking.",
  "Experiments": "We will use the CASF-2016 redocking set (285 complexes, 10 methods each) and the screening set (57 targets × 285 ligands, 5 batch methods). For each complex, we extract features: ligand torsions, molecular weight, logP, hydrogen bond donors/acceptors, topological polar surface area, number of rotatable bonds, and receptor pocket volume and hydrophobicity (computed from the binding site). We label each complex with the method that yields the lowest RMSD (redocking) or highest enrichment factor (screening). We train an XGBoost classifier to predict the best method for redocking, and a regression model to predict per-method enrichment scores for screening. We evaluate via 5-fold cross-validation on CASF-2016, measuring routing accuracy and the actual RMSD/enrichment obtained under a time budget (e.g., max allowed time per complex). We compare against: (1) the single best fixed method (Vina-GPU2), (2) the fastest method (QVina2-CUDA), (3) a static method that minimizes global error, and (4) an oracle that always picks the true best method. We also test generalizability on the CASF-2013 coreset. We further analyze feature importance to understand which molecular properties drive method preference, and simulate the policy under varying time budgets to quantify the improvement in the Pareto frontier.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The approach relies on the assumption that method superiority is consistent across similar complexes; the model may overfit to CASF-2016 and fail on novel chemistries. The feature representation may not capture all subtle factors (e.g., ligand flexibility beyond torsion count, receptor flexibility). The benefit might be modest if the top methods are strongly correlated in accuracy. Additionally, the policy may choose a suboptimal method due to prediction errors, potentially missing good poses. External validation is needed to confirm robustness."
}}
